<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pact of Biak-na-Bato</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pact of Biak-na-Bato was a truce agreed in December 1897 between the Spanish governor-general Fernando Primo de Rivera and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a>'s revolutionaries, mediated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-paterno">Pedro Paterno</a>. Aguinaldo and other leaders accepted exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> in return for payments and promised reforms. Neither side fully honoured it, and the revolution resumed in 1898 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish-American War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pact-of-biak-na-bato.jpg" alt="Pact of Biak-na-Bato" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Hongkong_Junta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The pact was signed on 14 December 1897 to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a>. It was concluded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-miguel-bulacan">San Miguel, Bulacan</a>, in the house of Pablo Tecson, a revolutionary captain who served as a brigadier general in the Brigada Del Pilar of General Gregorio del Pilar. Aguinaldo and his fellow leaders were granted amnesty and a money indemnity, and Aguinaldo had decided to use the money to buy firearms and ammunition for a later return to the islands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Writing in 1899, Aguinaldo said that he and any associates who wished could live in any foreign country, and that he chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>. An indemnity of $MXN800,000 was to be paid to him personally in three instalments: 400,000 when the arms at Biak-na-Bato were handed over, 200,000 when 800 stand of arms had been surrendered, and the rest at 1,000 stand, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a> sung in Manila Cathedral for the return of peace.</p><p>Two Spanish generals were to be held as hostages until he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and received the first payment. He added that the expulsion of the religious corporations had also been agreed, but that Primo de Rivera asked for it to be left out of the treaty to spare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> criticism.</p><p>The historian Teodoro Agoncillo describes three documents, known together as the Truce of Biak-na-Bato, which also promised a further $MXN900,000 to the families of non-combatant Filipinos who had suffered in the fighting. Sonia M. Zaide names them the Program, the Act of Agreement and a third document on the indemnity, a total of 1,700,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under the first part of the pact, Aguinaldo and twenty-five other leaders of the revolution left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> carrying $MXN400,000. The men who stayed behind received 200,000, but the third instalment was never paid. No general amnesty was ever declared, and sporadic skirmishes continued in the islands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pact of Biak-na-Bato” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Exhausted, both sides accepted the <b>Pact of Biak-na-Bato</b>, mediated by Pedro Paterno in December 1897.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-biak-na-bato">Republic of Biak-na-Bato</a></li></ul></section>
